          {
            "id": "7702812861063531798",
            "url": "https://www.tiktok.com/@agenticfact/video/7702812861063531798",
            "image": "https://p16-common-sign.tiktokcdn.com/tos-no1a-p-0037-no/oAQIlLVF5DeKL0GVgenCfIIAIC4P0yQjWvZAj4~tplv-tiktokx-cropcenter:500:800.jpeg?biz_tag=musically_video.video_cover&dr=14577&idc=my2&ps=13740610&refresh_token=d47080ab&shcp=9b759fb9&shp=c1333099&t=4d5b0474&x-expires=1795438800&x-signature=VHXhGoZnKWVLtHp1tijdfNt2pIo%3D",
            "likes": 3621,
            "saves": 2087,
            "views": 75793,
            "author": "agenticfact",
            "region": "ES",
            "shares": 205,
            "caption": "A research team built a fake trading firm staffed entirely by AIs, and they have to argue before every trade. Over a three-month test, Apple's stock dropped and the AIs still made 26%. The trick: no single AI makes the call, because one AI on its own tends to agree with you. You can do the same before any big decision, like a job offer: tell your AI to work as a team. Researchers who only gather facts and never decide. One argues for it, another against it, and each has to answer the other. A judge picks the stronger side (\"both have a point\" doesn't count). Three critics attack the plan: one who loves risk, one who worries and one who stays neutral. And a boss who can still say no, but has to write down what would change their mind. It's not about a smarter AI; it's about one that isn't allowed to simply agree.",
